<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edmund Andros</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1637">1637</a>–<a href="/years/1714">1714</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Sir Edmund Andros</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edmund Andros was an English colonial administrator who governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> from 1674 to 1681. In 1686 he became governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, which merged the northern colonies under royal rule without elected assemblies. His rule was deeply resented, and after news of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Glorious Revolution</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> rose and deposed him in 1689. He later governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edmund-andros.jpg" alt="Edmund Andros" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mary Beale · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Edmund_Andros_by_Beale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Andros was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 6 December 1637, the son of Amice Andros, Bailiff of Guernsey and a staunch supporter of Charles I, and Elizabeth Stone. In 1656 he was apprenticed to his uncle Sir Robert Stone, a cavalry captain, and he served in two winter campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, including the relief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> in 1659, gaining fluency in French, Swedish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>. On his father's death in 1674 he succeeded him as Bailiff of Guernsey, a post for life whose deputy, his uncle Charles Andros, left him free to travel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-england">James, Duke of York</a>, made him the first proprietary governor of the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, which took in the former New Netherland. In July 1675 he sailed to Saybrook to press the duke's claim against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>, found the fort held by Connecticut militia and went home. He then visited the Iroquois, agreed to go on supplying them with firearms and in 1677 negotiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covenant-chain">Covenant Chain</a>, a lasting peace. Opponents accused him of favouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> merchants and of trading for private gain, and in January 1681 he was recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><p>He reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> on 20 December 1686 as governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>, to rule with a council but no assembly. He took the keys of Samuel Willard's Third Church for Anglican services, revived old Massachusetts taxes and had officials of resisting towns arrested, limited town meetings to one a year, and declared land titles granted under the vacated charter void. In Hartford in October 1687, according to legend, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a>'s charter vanished into the Charter Oak. On 18 April 1689, after news of the overthrow of James II, Boston rose and Andros was arrested.</p><p>William III, who remembered that Andros had visited his court in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, approved of his service. When Lord Effingham resigned in February 1692, Andros was made governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-nicholson">Francis Nicholson</a>, who had wanted the post, became lieutenant governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>; their worsening quarrel complicated his rule. His recall was announced in May 1698, and Nicholson replaced him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Andros resumed his post as bailiff and was Lieutenant Governor of Guernsey from 1704 to 1708. He married three times and had no children. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> on 24 February 1714 and was buried at St Anne's Church, Soho. The historian Michael Kammen judged that he failed in all his colonial roles, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> leaves him off its list of colonial governors. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> he was recognised as an effective administrator who carried out his orders; the biographer Mary Lou Lustig called him an accomplished statesman whose style was often autocratic, arbitrary and dictatorial.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edmund Andros” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Through the Covenant Chain, the alliance made at Albany in 1677 with Governor <b>Edmund Andros</b> of New York and later extended to other colonies, they became the principal Native partner of the English and claimed authority over many dependent peoples, including the Delaware and remnant groups of the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…Evertsen and Binckes retook the colony, Anthony Colve governed it and the city was renamed New Orange, but the Republic, exhausted by the disaster years of 1672–1674, gave it up by the Treaty of Westminster in February 1674; on 10 November 1674 <b>Edmund Andros</b> took over from Colve.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-netherland">New Netherland · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Militia and citizens in Boston, prompted by news of the Glorious Revolution, rose against <b>Sir Edmund Andros</b>, governor of the Dominion of New England, and arrested dominion officials.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-18">18 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Joseph Dudley headed an interim government from 1686, and <b>Sir Edmund Andros</b> arrived in Boston as governor in December 1686.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-new-england">Dominion of New England</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1687" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Edmund Andros"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4993 5724h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4993 5724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=1687">What was here in 1687? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
